% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 2
% composition-elements: pitch (do-re-mi-so-la pentatonic with high do), duration (half notes and quarter notes), texture (melody over a repeating do-so bordun), form (AB, where B is A played exactly backwards, a mirror)
% composition-listen: Stella Chiweshe — "Chachimurenga", from the album Ambuya? (Piranha, 1987); linked upload is Piranha Records' remastered version from the 2021 reissue Ambuya!
% composition-listen-url: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2k9GM7vloIA
% composition-bridge: Follow one repeating interlocking figure while the voice moves independently; notice cyclical rather than chord-by-chord listening.
% composition-context: Stella Chiweshe (1946-2023) was one of the first women to become a professional player of the mbira dzavadzimu, the Shona lamellophone of Zimbabwe used in ceremonies that call on the ancestors, and she carried it to audiences worldwide. "Chachimurenga", the opening track of Ambuya? (1987), takes its name from chimurenga, the Shona word for struggle used for Zimbabwe's liberation wars. In mbira music interlocking parts repeat in cycles while voices weave over them. This original pentatonic school piece is not mbira repertoire.
% composition-distance: Original melody, words and bordun; no kushaura or kutsinhira (mbira lead or interlocking) part, lyric or recording phrase is transcribed. The mirror form is a classroom device, not a feature of mbira music.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"3-4","role":"bordun group","prompt":"How does the cycle change?","choices":["same","one rest","swap do and so"],"replyBars":2}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"5-8","role":"singers","prompt":"How does the second half go?","choices":["mirror, as written","sing the first half again","hold high do"],"replyBars":4}
% dossier-function: original Grade 2 AB song in which the second half is the first half sung backwards, over a do-so bordun that repeats like a cycle
% dossier-elements: eight bars in 4/4 with no pickup; pitch set C-D-E-G-A-C (do-re-mi-so-la-high do), written range C4-C5; half notes and quarter notes only; A (bars 1-4) climbs from do to high do in the rhythm half-quarter-quarter, quarter-quarter-half; B (bars 5-8) is the exact retrograde of A, every pitch and duration in reverse order, so it falls from high do back to do in the same rhythm; the mirror line falls between bars 4 and 5, where high do is sung twice; largest leap a perfect fourth (G4-C5, at bars 4 and 5)
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 88 is the target; begin at 76. The written range C4-C5 sits inside the young-unison C4-D5 range, and C major pentatonic (no F or B) is the rehearsal key, so barred instruments can be set up with F and B removed. Teach in steps: set up the do-so bordun (C and G together on beats 1 and 3) and keep it going as a cycle; sing A by echo, two bars at a time; write A's note names on the board, read them right to left and discover that this is B; sing the whole song over the bordun; then let the bordun group change its cycle with cue A
% dossier-accompaniment: The classroom accompaniment is a repeating do-so bordun on bass barred instruments, C and G together on beats 1 and 3. %%style drone is used only because the Band's drone holds an open do-so fifth, the same sound as the bordun, and never changes chord, which suits the cycle-not-chords listening; the page labels that style "tanpura and tabla", a South Asian sound, so present the Band as "drone" and never as mbira or Zimbabwean. No chord symbols are written, so the drone holds C and G throughout
% dossier-lyrics: Original English words written for this record, one syllable per note: "Up goes the pat-tern high, up to the moun-tain top; look in the mir-ror, see: back comes the pat-tern home." The words rise with A and fall with B; they are not themselves a palindrome. Sung throughout
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original composition for Grand Central Stationery, 11 November 2026, under human-directed grade, range and teaching constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community and not modelled on "Chachimurenga" or any mbira piece; the recording is a listening bridge only
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the melody, words and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. Stella Chiweshe's composition and the Piranha recording remain their owners' copyright and are not included; the class listens through the linked official upload
% dossier-respect: Name Stella Chiweshe, Zimbabwe and the Shona people, and the mbira dzavadzimu as an instrument of ceremony and history; mention that she played it when few women were allowed to. Do not detach mbira from Shona spiritual and living practice, do not market a xylophone bordun as "African mbira music", and do not call the instrument a "thumb piano" without its own name
% dossier-review: Grade 2 classroom music teacher, Zimbabwean mbira educator, second score reader; all reviews still required
