N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit: A: bar 1 C half, E-D quarters; bar 2 E-G quarters, A half; bar 3 G half, A-high C quarters; bar 4 A-G quarters, high C half. B: bar 5 high C half, G-A quarters; bar 6 high C-A quarters, G half; bar 7 A half, G-E quarters; bar 8 D-E quarters, C half. B is A's twelve notes read backwards, pitch and duration, and because each two-bar rhythm (half-quarter-quarter, quarter-quarter-half) is itself a palindrome, B keeps A's rhythm. Every bar totals four quarter beats; the only pitches are C, D, E, G, A and high C; range C4-C5; steps and thirds except the perfect fourth G4-C5 at bars 4 and 5; durations are halves and quarters only. The song ends on do. Originality: the do-re-mi-so-la-do climb is common property; the tune was checked against "Chachimurenga", "Hot Cross Buns" (B's close is re-mi-do, not mi-re-do), "Mary Had a Little Lamb" and "Kumbaya" and shares no phrase with them.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 2 children hear B as the mirror of A without seeing it written, whether the repeated high do at bars 4-5 is comfortable, and whether the bordun group can change its cycle on cue are untested. How much of chimurenga's history to share with seven-year-olds is for the mbira educator and teacher. A classroom trial, a Zimbabwean mbira educator and a second score reader remain required.
